<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/9th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Meerssen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/870">870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Meerssen was concluded in August 870 by the Carolingian kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>, who divided between them the Middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdom</a> of Lotharingia after the death of their nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a>. Louis gained the eastern part, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, and Charles the western part. The division was largely revised in Louis's line's favour by the Treaty of Ribemont in 880.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-meerssen.jpg" alt="Treaty of Meerssen" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Furfur · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vertrag_von_Meerssen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty was the third of the partitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> between August 843 and August 870, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-prum">Treaty of Prüm</a>. Verdun had divided the empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> among his sons: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair I</a> received the imperial crown and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a>, Louis the German East Francia, and Charles the Bald West Francia. When Lothair I died in 855, Prüm split Middle Francia among his sons: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-italy">Louis II of Italy</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, Charles of Provence became king of Provence, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burgundy">Upper Burgundy</a>, a realm that came to be called Lotharingia.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a> died in 869 without legitimate children. His heir was his brother Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-italy">Louis II of Italy</a>, who was then campaigning against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>, so their uncles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> took the inheritance. Charles had himself crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> that year, but his brother forced him to share, as they had agreed at Metz in 868.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty, concluded on 8 August 870, divided the northern half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a>, stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhone</a> valley to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, into two larger eastern and western divisions. The border ran roughly along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ourthe">Ourthe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> and the Rhone. In the north Louis received most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a>'s Austrasia, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a>, though large parts of the Frisian coast were under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> control and were divided only on paper.</p><p>In the south Louis received most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burgundy">Upper Burgundy</a> left to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a>, while Charles received Lothair's inheritance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burgundy">Lower Burgundy</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a>, and a small western part of Upper Burgundy, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besancon">Besançon</a>, which opened his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Louis joined the newly acquired parts of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a> to the subkingdom of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-younger">Louis the Younger</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a>'s illegitimate son Hugh was granted the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The arrangement did not last ten years. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> died in 876, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>, by then king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and emperor, attacked eastern Lotharingia but was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-younger">Louis the Younger</a> at the Battle of Andernach in 876. In 879 Louis the Younger campaigned in western Lotharingia, and Charles's grandsons were forced to cede the whole of it to him, as sealed by the Treaty of Ribemont in 880, by which it finally became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Meerssen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He ruled them from palaces such as Regensburg and Frankfurt, and in 870 the <b>Treaty of Meerssen</b> added much of Lotharingia.</blockquote><a href="/state/east-francia">East Francia</a></li><li><blockquote>After Lothair II died in 869 his uncles divided the kingdom at <b>Meerssen</b> in 870, and in 880 all of it passed to East Francia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lotharingia">Kingdom of Lotharingia</a></li></ul></section>
